.TITLE "systemctl"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
Controls the systemd system and service manager. It is the modern way to __start__, __stop__, and __manage__ background services on most Linux distributions.

.DIV "TLDR"
**systemctl start service**
Starts a specific background service immediately.
> __start__ subcommand | __service__ the name of the service

**systemctl stop service**
Stops a currently running background service.
> __stop__ subcommand | __service__ the name of the service

**systemctl restart service**
Stops and then immediately starts a service, often used to apply configuration changes.
> __restart__ subcommand | __service__ the name of the service

**systemctl status service**
Displays the current state and recent log entries for a specific service.
> __status__ subcommand | __service__ the name of the service

**systemctl enable service**
Configures a service to start automatically whenever the system boots up.
> __enable__ subcommand | __service__ the name of the service

**systemctl disable service**
Prevents a service from starting automatically during the boot process.
> __disable__ subcommand | __service__ the name of the service

.DIV "SUMMARY"
Managing services usually requires __root privileges__ via __sudo__. If a service fails to start, use __systemctl status__ or __journalctl -u service__ to inspect the error messages and diagnose the issue.

.DIV "SEE ALSO"
**linux:journalctl** **linux:service** **linux:chkconfig**

